@subject acfbe
@chapter 14b
@title Sales Variances and Operating Statements
@lo E3
@topic t1 | Sales variances | E3
@topic t2 | Operating statements | E3
@topic t3 | Deriving actual data | E3
@topic t4 | Control action | E3

=== Q001
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: The standard selling price is $25. 1,200 units were sold for $31,200. What is the selling price variance ($)? (It is favourable.)
answer: 1200
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Selling price variance = actual revenue - (actual units sold x standard price)
step: Standard revenue for actual sales = 1,200 x 25 = 30,000.
step: 31,200 - 30,000 = $1,200 F.
check: 31200-1200*25
explain: The actual price was $26 per unit.
mistake: Using budgeted units.
tip: Actual units sold.
src: Study text ch14b - selling price variance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Under ABSORPTION costing, at what value is the sales volume variance calculated?
+ Standard profit per unit || Sales volume profit variance.
- Standard selling price per unit || Overstates the effect.
- Standard contribution per unit || Marginal costing.
- Actual profit per unit || Standards are used.
explain: Under marginal costing, standard contribution is used.
mistake: Valuing at selling price.
tip: Absorption = profit.
src: Study text ch14b - volume variance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Budgeted sales were 5,000 units and actual sales were 4,600 units. Standard profit is $6 per unit. What is the sales volume profit variance ($)? (It is adverse.)
answer: 2400
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Sales volume profit variance = (actual units - budgeted units) x standard profit per unit
step: Shortfall = 4,600 - 5,000 = -400 units.
step: Variance = 400 x 6 = $2,400 A.
check: (5000-4600)*6
explain: Fewer units sold means less standard profit earned.
mistake: Using selling price.
tip: Units x standard profit.
src: Study text ch14b - volume variance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What does an operating statement do?
+ Reconciles budgeted profit with actual profit through the variances || Explains the difference.
- Lists the company's assets || That is a SOFP.
- Shows only cash flows || Cash budget.
- Records sales invoices || Not its role.
explain: Favourable variances are added and adverse variances deducted.
mistake: Thinking it shows only costs.
tip: Budget to actual, via variances.
src: Study text ch14b - operating statement (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which variance does NOT appear in a MARGINAL costing operating statement?
+ Fixed overhead volume variance || No overhead is absorbed under marginal costing.
- Fixed overhead expenditure variance || It does appear.
- Sales volume contribution variance || It does appear.
- Material price variance || It does appear.
explain: Marginal statements value volume at contribution and have no FOH volume variance.
mistake: Including a volume variance.
tip: Marginal = no FOH volume.
src: Study text ch14b - marginal statements (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Standard selling price is $30 and standard variable cost is $18. Budgeted sales were 5,000 units and actual sales 4,600 units. What is the sales volume contribution variance ($)? (It is adverse.)
answer: 4800
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Volume contribution variance = (actual units - budgeted units) x standard contribution per unit
step: Standard contribution = 30 - 18 = $12.
step: Variance = 400 x 12 = $4,800 A.
check: (5000-4600)*(30-18)
explain: This is used in marginal costing operating statements.
mistake: Using standard profit.
tip: Marginal = contribution.
src: Study text ch14b - volume contribution (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which quantity is used in the selling price variance?
+ Actual quantity sold || Isolates the price effect on actual sales.
- Budgeted quantity || Used in the volume variance.
- Quantity produced || Sales variances use sales.
- Standard quantity of materials || Not related.
explain: Price variance = (actual price - standard price) x actual quantity sold.
mistake: Using budgeted quantity.
tip: Price x actual sold.
src: Study text ch14b - price variance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: When should a variance be investigated?
+ When the expected benefits of investigating and correcting it exceed the costs || Cost-benefit rule.
- Always, whatever the size || Inefficient.
- Never || Loses control.
- Only when it is favourable || Both types matter.
explain: Control limits and trends help identify significant variances.
mistake: Investigating everything.
tip: Benefit > cost.
src: Study text ch14b - control action (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: In an operating statement, how are ADVERSE variances treated?
+ Deducted from budgeted (or standard) profit || They reduce profit.
- Added to budgeted profit || Favourable variances are added.
- Ignored || They are included.
- Shown only in a note || They are in the reconciliation.
explain: The statement ends at actual profit.
mistake: Adding adverse variances.
tip: A = subtract; F = add.
src: Study text ch14b - operating statement (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: What action is appropriate for a variance caused by random, chance fluctuation?
+ No action - a standard is an average within a normal range || Nothing to correct.
- Dismiss the manager responsible || Not caused by performance.
- Revise the standard immediately || Not needed for random effects.
- Change the recording system || Not a measurement error.
explain: Different causes call for different actions.
mistake: Over-reacting to random variances.
tip: Random = leave it.
src: Study text ch14b - control action (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: The standard price is $40. 3,000 units were sold for $119,100. What is the selling price variance?
+ $900 adverse || Standard revenue 120,000 vs actual 119,100.
- $900 favourable || Revenue was lower than standard.
- $119,100 favourable || Actual revenue.
- $3,000 adverse || Not supported.
formula: Price variance = actual revenue - actual units x standard price
step: Standard revenue = 3,000 x 40 = 120,000.
step: 119,100 - 120,000 = -900: $900 A.
check: 120000-119100
explain: The actual price was $39.70.
mistake: Using budgeted units.
tip: Lower price = adverse.
src: Study text ch14b - price variance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q012
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: A company cuts its selling price to increase sales. What variance pattern is MOST likely?
+ Adverse selling price variance and favourable sales volume variance || Interdependence.
- Favourable price and favourable volume || The price is lower.
- Adverse price and adverse volume || Volume should rise.
- No variances || Prices changed.
explain: The two variances should be judged together.
mistake: Judging the price variance alone.
tip: Price down, volume up.
src: Study text ch14b - interdependence (concept basis; original question)

=== Q013
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: The labour rate variance was $2,600 adverse on 13,000 hours paid. The standard rate is $8.00 per hour. What was the actual rate per hour ($)?
answer: 8.2
tol: 0.001
unit: $
formula: Actual rate = standard rate + adverse variance / hours paid
step: Variance per hour = 2,600 / 13,000 = $0.20.
step: Actual rate = 8.00 + 0.20 = $8.20.
check: 8+2600/13000
explain: Adverse means the actual rate is higher.
mistake: Subtracting the variance.
tip: Adverse = add.
src: Study text ch14b - deriving data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q014
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: The sales volume profit variance was $1,500 favourable. Standard profit is $5 per unit and actual sales were 2,300 units. What were budgeted sales (units)?
answer: 2000
tol: 0
unit: units
formula: Budgeted units = actual units - favourable variance / standard profit
step: Extra units sold = 1,500 / 5 = 300.
step: Budget = 2,300 - 300 = 2,000 units.
check: 2300-1500/5
explain: Favourable volume means actual sales exceeded budget.
mistake: Adding the 300 units.
tip: Favourable = actual above budget.
src: Study text ch14b - deriving data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q015
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Absorption costing. Budgeted profit $30,000. Sales volume profit variance $2,400 A. Selling price variance $1,500 F. Cost variances: material $800 A; labour $1,100 F; variable overhead $300 A; fixed overhead expenditure $2,000 A; fixed overhead volume $1,200 A.
q: What is the actual profit?
+ $25,900 || 30,000 - 2,400 + 1,500 - 800 + 1,100 - 300 - 2,000 - 1,200.
- $34,100 || Signs reversed.
- $27,100 || Omits the volume variance.
- $27,600 || Standard profit on actual sales.
formula: Actual profit = budgeted profit + F variances - A variances
step: Standard profit on actual sales = 30,000 - 2,400 = 27,600; add price 1,500 = 29,100.
step: Cost variances net = -800 + 1,100 - 300 - 2,000 - 1,200 = -3,200; actual = 25,900.
check: 30000-2400+1500-800+1100-300-2000-1200
explain: Working down an operating statement step by step avoids sign errors.
mistake: Adding adverse variances.
tip: F add, A subtract.
src: Study text ch14b - operating statement (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q016
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO are differences between a marginal and an absorption costing operating statement?
+ The sales volume variance is valued at standard contribution, not standard profit || Marginal uses contribution.
+ There is no fixed overhead volume variance || Only expenditure appears.
- Material variances are excluded || They appear in both.
- The selling price variance is calculated differently || It is the same in both.
explain: Only a minority of candidates remembered these differences in a past exam.
mistake: Including a volume variance in marginal statements.
tip: Contribution, no volume.
src: Study text ch14b - statements (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Match each cause of a variance with the appropriate action.
pair: Measurement error => Correct the recording system
pair: Out-of-date standard => Revise the standard
pair: Inefficient operations => Take corrective action
pair: Random fluctuation => No action needed
explain: The right response depends on the cause.
mistake: Blaming staff for an out-of-date standard.
tip: Fix the cause, not the symptom.
src: Study text ch14b - control action (concept basis; original question)

=== Q018
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: Budgeted profit is $30,000 and the sales volume profit variance is $2,400 adverse. What is the standard profit on actual sales ($)?
answer: 27600
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Standard profit on actual sales = budgeted profit +/- sales volume profit variance
step: Budgeted profit = 30,000.
step: Less adverse volume variance 2,400 = $27,600.
check: 30000-2400
explain: This is the first sub-total in an absorption operating statement.
mistake: Adding the adverse variance.
tip: Budget, then volume.
src: Study text ch14b - statement layout (concept basis; original question)

=== Q019
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Why is the sales volume variance NOT valued at selling price?
+ Selling more units also incurs more cost, so the profit effect is measured by profit or contribution per unit || Revenue alone overstates the effect.
- Because selling price is unknown || It is known.
- Because volume variances are always zero || Not true.
- Because selling price is a fixed cost || Not a cost.
explain: Absorption uses standard profit; marginal uses standard contribution.
mistake: Valuing at selling price.
tip: Volume x margin, not price.
src: Study text ch14b - volume variance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q020
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: The selling price variance was $900 adverse on 3,000 units sold. The standard price is $40. What was the actual selling price per unit?
+ $39.70 || 40 - 900 / 3,000.
- $40.30 || Adds the variance.
- $39.10 || Not supported.
- $37.00 || Not supported.
formula: Actual price = standard price - adverse variance / units sold
step: Variance per unit = 900 / 3,000 = 0.30.
step: Actual price = 40 - 0.30 = $39.70.
check: 40-900/3000
explain: Adverse price variance = lower actual price.
mistake: Adding the variance.
tip: Adverse price = cheaper sale.
src: Study text ch14b - deriving data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: A variance arises because a clerk recorded material issues to the wrong job. What action is appropriate?
+ Correct the recording system and procedures || It is a measurement error.
- Revise the standard || The standard is fine.
- Penalise the production team || Not their performance.
- Ignore it permanently || Errors should be fixed.
explain: Measurement errors distort variances without any real performance issue.
mistake: Treating recording errors as inefficiency.
tip: Bad data = fix data.
src: Study text ch14b - measurement errors (concept basis; original question)

=== Q022
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Put the lines of an absorption costing operating statement in order.
item: Budgeted profit
item: Sales volume profit variance
item: Standard profit on actual sales
item: Selling price variance
item: Cost variances
item: Actual profit
explain: Cost variances include material, labour, overhead expenditure and volume.
mistake: Placing the price variance before standard profit on actual sales.
tip: Budget, volume, standard, price, costs, actual.
src: Study text ch14b - statement layout (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Marginal costing. Budgeted profit $30,000. Sales volume contribution variance $4,800 A. Selling price variance $1,500 F. Material $800 A; labour $1,100 F; variable overhead $300 A; fixed overhead expenditure $2,000 A.
q: What is the actual profit ($)?
answer: 24700
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Actual profit = budgeted profit + F - A variances (no volume variance)
step: 30,000 - 4,800 + 1,500 = 26,700.
step: 26,700 - 800 + 1,100 - 300 - 2,000 = 24,700.
check: 30000-4800+1500-800+1100-300-2000
explain: No fixed overhead volume variance appears under marginal costing.
mistake: Adding a volume variance.
tip: Marginal: contribution volume, no FOH volume.
src: Study text ch14b - marginal statement (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What could cause an ADVERSE sales volume variance?
+ A competitor launching a cheaper product, reducing demand || Fewer units sold.
- An increase in selling price with no change in demand || Price variance.
- A fall in material prices || Cost variance.
- Selling more units than budgeted || Favourable.
explain: Economic conditions and marketing also affect volume.
mistake: Linking cost changes to sales volume.
tip: Fewer sold = adverse volume.
src: Study text ch14b - causes (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: How can control limits help decide whether to investigate a variance?
+ Variances outside a set range (eg ±5% of standard) are treated as significant || Focuses attention.
- They remove all variances || Not possible.
- They set the selling price || Not related.
- They replace standards || Standards are still needed.
explain: Trends of small variances in one direction may also be worth investigating.
mistake: Investigating every small variance.
tip: Outside the limits = look.
src: Study text ch14b - control limits (concept basis; original question)

=== Q026
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: The material price variance was $1,560 favourable on 7,800 kg bought. The standard price is $5.00 per kg. What was the actual price per kg ($)?
answer: 4.8
tol: 0.001
unit: $
formula: Actual price = standard price - favourable variance / quantity
step: Variance per kg = 1,560 / 7,800 = 0.20.
step: Actual price = 5.00 - 0.20 = $4.80.
check: 5-1560/7800
explain: Favourable means the actual price was lower.
mistake: Adding the variance.
tip: Favourable price = cheaper.
src: Study text ch14b - deriving data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t1
status: review
q: A trainee calculates the selling price variance using BUDGETED sales quantity. What is wrong?
+ It should use ACTUAL quantity sold, so price and volume effects are not mixed || Volume is separate.
- Nothing is wrong || It mixes effects.
- It should use quantity produced || Sales variances use sales.
- It should use standard profit || That is volume.
explain: The volume variance captures the effect of quantity.
mistake: Using budgeted quantity.
tip: Price variance = actual sold.
src: Study text ch14b - price variance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Material prices have risen 15% across the market since standards were set. A large adverse price variance results. What is the BEST response?
+ Revise the standard so future variances reflect controllable performance || The standard is out of date.
- Discipline the buyer || Not their fault.
- Ignore it forever || Outdated standards mislead.
- Change the recording system || Not a measurement error.
explain: The variance is due to a planning error, not operations.
mistake: Blaming buyers for inflation.
tip: Old standard = revise it.
src: Study text ch14b - out-of-date standards (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q029
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Budgeted sales were 8,000 units at a standard contribution of $13.40 per unit. Actual sales were 8,500 units. What is the sales volume contribution variance ($)? (It is favourable.)
answer: 6700
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Variance = (actual units - budgeted units) x standard contribution
step: Extra units = 8,500 - 8,000 = 500.
step: Variance = 500 x 13.40 = $6,700 F.
check: (8500-8000)*13.4
explain: More units sold earn more standard contribution.
mistake: Using selling price.
tip: Units x contribution.
src: Study text ch14b - volume contribution (concept basis; original question)

=== Q030
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Under marginal costing, how are fixed overheads treated in the operating statement?
+ Budgeted fixed overhead is deducted from contribution, and only the expenditure variance is shown || No volume variance.
- They are absorbed into product costs || Absorption costing.
- They are ignored entirely || They are included.
- A volume variance is calculated || Not under marginal costing.
explain: Fixed costs are treated as period costs.
mistake: Absorbing fixed costs.
tip: Marginal = period cost.
src: Study text ch14b - marginal statement (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Standard selling price $50, standard full cost $38. Budgeted sales 2,000 units; actual sales 1,850 units at $52. What are the selling price and sales volume profit variances?
+ Price $3,700 F; volume $1,800 A || 1,850 x 2 and 150 x 12.
- Price $4,000 F; volume $1,800 A || Price uses budgeted units.
- Price $3,700 F; volume $7,500 A || Volume at selling price.
- Price $3,700 A; volume $1,800 F || Signs reversed.
formula: Price = (AP - SP) x actual units; volume = (actual - budget) x standard profit
step: Price = (52 - 50) x 1,850 = 3,700 F.
step: Volume = (1,850 - 2,000) x (50 - 38) = 150 x 12 = 1,800 A.
check: (52-50)*1850
explain: Higher prices may have reduced volume - interdependence.
mistake: Valuing volume at selling price.
tip: Price on actual; volume on profit.
src: Study text ch14b - sales variances (concept basis; original question)

=== Q032
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: When working back from a variance to an actual figure, which relationship is used?
+ Variance = standard - actual (for costs), so actual = standard -/+ variance || Rearrange the formula.
- Variance = actual x standard || Not correct.
- Actual = variance only || Incomplete.
- Standard = variance x 2 || Not correct.
explain: Use a symbol for the unknown and solve.
mistake: Applying the sign the wrong way.
tip: Adverse cost: actual > standard.
src: Study text ch14b - deriving data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q033
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Absorption costing. Budget: 4,000 units at a standard price of $45 and standard full cost of $33. Actual: 4,200 units sold for $187,950. Total cost variances are $3,100 adverse.
q: What is the actual profit ($)?
answer: 46250
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Actual = budgeted profit + volume variance + price variance - net adverse cost variances
step: Budgeted profit = 4,000 x 12 = 48,000; volume = 200 x 12 = 2,400 F; standard profit on actual sales = 50,400.
step: Price = 187,950 - 4,200 x 45 = 187,950 - 189,000 = 1,050 A.
step: Actual = 50,400 - 1,050 - 3,100 = $46,250.
check: 4000*12+200*12+(187950-4200*45)-3100
explain: Each line can be checked independently.
mistake: Valuing volume at price.
tip: Build up the statement line by line.
src: Study text ch14b - operating statement (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q034
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Standard: price $20, variable cost $12, fixed cost $5 per unit (based on budgeted output and sales of 6,000 units). Actual sales and production 5,500 units.
q: What are the sales volume variances under absorption and marginal costing?
+ Absorption $1,500 A; marginal $4,000 A || 500 x $3 profit; 500 x $8 contribution.
- Absorption $4,000 A; marginal $1,500 A || Reversed.
- Both $10,000 A || At selling price.
- Both $1,500 A || Marginal uses contribution.
formula: Volume = (actual - budget) x standard profit (absorption) or contribution (marginal)
step: Standard profit = 20 - 12 - 5 = 3; contribution = 20 - 12 = 8.
step: Absorption = 500 x 3 = 1,500 A; marginal = 500 x 8 = 4,000 A.
check: 500*3
explain: The difference (2,500) is matched by the FOH volume variance under absorption.
mistake: Using one rate for both.
tip: Profit vs contribution.
src: Study text ch14b - comparison (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q035
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t1
status: review
q: The sales manager is praised for a $5,000 favourable selling price variance, but the sales volume variance is $9,000 adverse after a price rise. What is the BEST assessment?
+ The price rise appears to have reduced overall profit by $4,000, so it may not have been a good decision || Look at both variances.
- The sales manager performed well because of the favourable price variance || Ignores volume.
- The volume variance is unrelated to the price rise || Likely related.
- Both variances should be ignored || They matter.
explain: Interdependent variances must be judged together.
mistake: Praising one variance in isolation.
tip: Net effect matters.
src: Study text ch14b - interdependence (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q036
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Standard: 3 kg per unit at $6 per kg. Actual output 2,000 units. Material usage variance $1,800 favourable.
q: How many kg were actually used?
answer: 5700
tol: 0
unit: kg
formula: Actual kg = standard kg for actual output - favourable variance / standard price
step: Standard kg = 2,000 x 3 = 6,000; kg saved = 1,800 / 6 = 300.
step: Actual = 6,000 - 300 = 5,700 kg.
check: 2000*3-1800/6
explain: Favourable usage means less material than standard.
mistake: Adding the saving.
tip: Favourable usage = fewer kg.
src: Study text ch14b - deriving data (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q037
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO factors suggest a variance SHOULD be investigated?
+ It has been adverse and growing for several months || A trend.
+ It is large relative to the standard and the cost is controllable || Material and controllable.
- It is tiny and appears randomly || Not significant.
- It arises from a known, temporary measurement problem already fixed || Already dealt with.
explain: Trends and materiality are key signals.
mistake: Ignoring trends of small variances.
tip: Big, controllable or trending.
src: Study text ch14b - investigation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q038
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Standard labour: 2 hours per unit at $10 per hour. Actual: 1,000 units made; labour rate variance $450 adverse; labour efficiency variance $600 favourable; no idle time.
q: What was the actual labour cost ($)?
answer: 19850
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Actual cost = standard cost of actual output + adverse - favourable variances
step: Standard cost = 1,000 x 2 x 10 = 20,000.
step: Actual = 20,000 + 450 - 600 = $19,850.
check: 1000*2*10+450-600
explain: Hours worked = 2,000 - 60 = 1,940; actual rate = 19,850 / 1,940.
mistake: Applying the signs the wrong way.
tip: Adverse adds to cost; favourable reduces it.
src: Study text ch14b - deriving data (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: In a marginal costing operating statement, a trainee starts with budgeted profit, then values the sales volume variance at standard PROFIT per unit. What is wrong?
+ Under marginal costing, sales volume is valued at standard CONTRIBUTION per unit || Fixed costs don't vary with volume.
- Nothing is wrong || Wrong basis.
- Volume should be valued at selling price || Overstates.
- There should be no volume variance at all || The sales volume variance does exist.
explain: Only the FOH volume variance disappears under marginal costing; the sales volume variance remains.
mistake: Mixing the two systems.
tip: Marginal = contribution.
src: Study text ch14b - marginal statement (concept basis; original question)

=== Q040
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Why do actual profits under absorption and marginal costing differ in operating statements when production and sales volumes differ?
+ Absorption costing carries fixed overhead in inventory, while marginal costing charges it in full to the period || Inventory valuation differs.
- Selling prices differ between the methods || Prices are the same.
- Material costs differ || Same.
- They never differ || They can.
explain: When production equals sales, the two profits are the same.
mistake: Thinking the methods always agree.
tip: Inventory change = profit difference.
src: Study text ch14b - absorption vs marginal (concept basis; original question)

=== Q041
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
scenario: Budget: 10,000 units at $18, standard variable cost $10.50. Actual: 9,400 units sold for $172,020.
q: What is the TOTAL of the selling price and sales volume contribution variances ($)? (It is adverse.)
answer: 1680
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Total = price variance + volume contribution variance
step: Price = 172,020 - 9,400 x 18 = 172,020 - 169,200 = 2,820 F.
step: Volume = (9,400 - 10,000) x (18 - 10.50) = 600 x 7.50 = 4,500 A.
step: Net = 4,500 A - 2,820 F = $1,680 A.
check: 4500-(172020-9400*18)
explain: The higher price ($18.30) did not make up for the lost volume.
mistake: Netting variances with the wrong signs.
tip: Net F against A.
src: Study text ch14b - sales variances (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q042
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Why might a large FAVOURABLE variance be worth investigating?
+ It may reveal a standard that is too loose, or good practice worth copying elsewhere || Favourable is not always "good news".
- Favourable variances never need investigation || They can be informative.
- It always means fraud || Not necessarily.
- It shows the standard is perfect || Suggests the opposite.
explain: Both adverse and favourable variances can signal problems with standards.
mistake: Only investigating adverse variances.
tip: Big either way = look.
src: Study text ch14b - investigation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q043
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: The standard selling price is $15. 4,000 units were sold and the selling price variance was $2,000 adverse. What was the actual sales revenue ($)?
answer: 58000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Actual revenue = actual units x standard price - adverse price variance
step: Standard revenue = 4,000 x 15 = 60,000.
step: Actual = 60,000 - 2,000 = $58,000.
check: 4000*15-2000
explain: The actual price was $14.50.
mistake: Adding an adverse sales variance.
tip: Adverse sales = less revenue.
src: Study text ch14b - deriving data (concept basis; original question)

=== Q044
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Budgeted profit $50,000. Variances: sales volume profit $3,000 F; selling price $2,500 A; material $1,200 A; labour $800 F; fixed overhead expenditure $4,100 A.
q: What is the actual profit ($)?
answer: 46000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Actual profit = budgeted profit + F - A
step: 50,000 + 3,000 - 2,500 = 50,500.
step: 50,500 - 1,200 + 800 - 4,100 = $46,000.
check: 50000+3000-2500-1200+800-4100
explain: Net variances = 4,000 A.
mistake: Reversing signs.
tip: Add F, subtract A.
src: Study text ch14b - operating statement (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q045
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t1
status: review
q: A company switches to a premium material and raises its price. Results show a favourable selling price variance and an adverse material price variance. How should these be viewed?
+ Together, as one decision caused both; the net effect on profit is what matters || Interdependence.
- Separately, rewarding sales and blaming purchasing || Ignores the link.
- As unrelated random events || Clearly linked.
- Only the material variance matters || Both matter.
explain: Responsibility should reflect the decision that caused both.
mistake: Judging departments in isolation.
tip: One decision, two variances.
src: Study text ch14b - interdependence (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q046
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Standard selling price is $60 and standard full cost is $48. Budgeted sales were 1,500 units and actual sales 1,620 units. What is the sales volume profit variance ($)? (It is favourable.)
answer: 1440
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Variance = (actual - budgeted units) x standard profit
step: Standard profit = 60 - 48 = 12; extra units = 120.
step: Variance = 120 x 12 = $1,440 F.
check: (1620-1500)*(60-48)
explain: Standard profit is used under absorption costing.
mistake: Using selling price ($7,200).
tip: Extra units x standard profit.
src: Study text ch14b - volume variance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q047
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: Investigating a variance would cost $3,000. If a problem is found, correcting it would save an estimated $5,000, but there is only a 40% chance a correctable problem exists. Should it be investigated?
+ No - the expected saving of $2,000 is less than the $3,000 cost || 40% x 5,000 = 2,000.
- Yes - the saving of $5,000 exceeds the cost || Ignores probability.
- Yes - all variances must be investigated || Not cost-effective.
- No - variances are never investigated || Not the rule.
formula: Expected benefit = probability x saving; compare with cost
step: Expected saving = 40% x 5,000 = 2,000.
step: 2,000 < 3,000 cost: do not investigate.
check: 0.4*5000
explain: Investigate only when expected benefits exceed costs.
mistake: Ignoring probability.
tip: Expected benefit vs cost.
src: Study text ch14b - cost-benefit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which variance is calculated in exactly the same way in BOTH absorption and marginal costing operating statements?
+ Selling price variance || Price x actual units in both.
- Sales volume variance || Profit vs contribution.
- Fixed overhead volume variance || Absorption only.
- Fixed overhead total variance || Differs between the systems.
explain: Cost variances for materials, labour and VOH are also the same.
mistake: Thinking every variance changes.
tip: Price variance is common to both.
src: Study text ch14b - statements (concept basis; original question)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t1
status: review
important: 1
q: Which formula gives the selling price variance?
+ (Actual price - standard price) x actual quantity sold || Core formula.
- (Actual units - budgeted units) x standard profit || Volume variance.
- (Actual price - standard price) x budgeted quantity || Wrong quantity.
- Actual revenue - budgeted revenue || Mixes price and volume.
explain: Sales volume variance = (actual - budget units) x standard profit or contribution.
mistake: Using budgeted quantity.
tip: Price x actual sold.
src: Study text ch14b - price variance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t2
status: review
important: 1
q: Which pair describes the two key differences in a MARGINAL costing operating statement?
+ Sales volume variance at standard contribution, and no fixed overhead volume variance || Remember both.
- No selling price variance, and no material variances || These appear.
- Sales volume at selling price, and no labour variances || Wrong.
- Fixed overhead volume variance only, and no expenditure variance || Reversed.
explain: Under marginal costing, actual fixed overhead is deducted in full.
mistake: Including a FOH volume variance.
tip: Contribution, no volume.
src: Study text ch14b - marginal statement (concept basis; original question)
